<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Johann Friedrich Struensee</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1737">1737</a>–<a href="/years/1772">1772</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Johann Friedrich Struensee was a German physician who became royal doctor to the mentally ill Christian VII of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> and the lover of Queen Caroline Matilda. From 1770 to 1772 he ruled Denmark-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> in the king's name, issuing hundreds of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a> decrees, including the abolition of censorship. Overthrown by a palace coup in January 1772, he was executed that April.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/johann-friedrich-struensee.jpg" alt="Johann Friedrich Struensee" width="320" height="383" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jens Juel · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Struensee_Juel_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Absolutism allowed rapid reform but also abuse: in 1770–1772 the royal physician <b>Johann Friedrich Struensee</b> ruled in the name of the mentally ill Christian VII, issuing hundreds of decrees, including freedom of the press, before he was overthrown and executed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/denmark-norway">Denmark-Norway · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>…out in the Austrian Netherlands and Hungary; Spain relapsed after the death of Charles III; and in Denmark <b>Johann Friedrich Struensee</b>, the royal physician who governed for the mentally ill Christian VII and issued more than a thousand decrees in little more than a year, was overthrown and…</blockquote><a href="/government/absolute-monarchy">Absolute monarchy · Weaknesses</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/halle-saale">Halle (Saale)</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/denmark">Denmark</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.48,11.97&amp;year=1759" aria-label="Halle (Saale) on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4821 381 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Johann Friedrich Struensee"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5271 651h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5271 651h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.48,11.97&amp;year=1759">What was here in 1759? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
